The transformation brief's last page says DO NOT BUILD FIRST, UNDERSTAND
FIRST, and its PATCH GROUP 0 is an audit with no production changes. This
is that patch. Four parallel read-only agents over apps/web, packages,
apps/admin, apps/mobile, backend, scripts and prisma; every load-bearing
claim re-verified by hand against the file before it was written down.

THE FINDING

Ecowoods has a real commercial pipeline. Quote converts to project
(lib/actions/quotes.ts:267), project generates staged invoices, Stripe's
signature-verified webhook writes Payment, JobOutcome captures actuals and
closes the matching Prediction. A customer portal exists at (portal)/mypage
with six real pages, and anonymous quotes attach themselves to an account
on email verification (lib/actions/auth.ts:159-163). The brief assumes far
less exists than does.

And none of it can be attributed to anything.

There is no visitor id, no session id, no UTM capture. Verified: the only
utm_ strings in the entire repository are outbound credit links to Unsplash
in RotatingBackground.tsx:90,120 — the site attributes other people and
captures nothing for itself. The `design` field that reaches /api/leads is
a deterministic configuration fingerprint, so two visitors who pick the same
floor produce the identical string; it cannot join a lead to a session. It
lands in free-text QuoteRequest.notes, and no code under app/admin/ decodes
it — studio-config.ts:330 says so in its own comment: "the desk pastes it
back into /floor-studio and sees the floor."

Of the brief's 21 canonical funnel events, 6 exist, 6 are partial, 9 are
missing. The last five — appointment, estimate, acceptance, deposit,
job_complete — are structurally unreachable: they are server writes, and
track() returns early on the server (lib/analytics.ts:97).

WHAT I WOULD HAVE BROKEN

FloorRecord — the Floor Passport the brief asks us to build — is already in
the schema, well designed, and has never held a row: zero create/update/
upsert calls in the whole repository. JobOutcome, which the brief asks us to
build for Program 2, is live and wired. Building either from scratch would
have duplicated working infrastructure, which is the brief's Rule #5.

THE ONE THAT LIES

SpecSheet.tsx:97 builds `/#quote?spec=...` — the query string placed after
the hash, so it is part of the fragment and is silently discarded on
arrival. It does not visibly break only because :62-69 re-saves to
localStorage as a side effect. And :156 fires design_handoff on that click,
so analytics records a successful handoff on the one CTA that loses its
payload. A metric that lies in the optimistic direction is worse than no
metric, and this is the first thing PATCH GROUP 1 fixes.

DELIVERED

All ten required deliverables: website and repository maps, capability
matrix in the seven specified states, duplication report, data-flow map
(seven breaks, five of them joins never made between systems that already
exist), four-program gap analysis, monetization model, priority matrix,
patch roadmap, success measurement plan.

The success test is a question the business cannot answer today at any
price: of the last 100 deposits, how many began in Floor Studio, and what
was the margin on those versus the rest.

Verified: repo hygiene, links (46 routes, 0 orphans), markdown editions
(20 surfaces), claims, business facts (479 files + 11 PDFs), pricing source
— all pass. No production code touched.

